course: dictionary
lesson: 2005
title: Concrete: a material or a meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «A house's foundations are concrete under the walls» is about a building material, a hard mix that holds up buildings.
explain: «Reinforced concrete has steel bars inside it so it can resist stretching as well as squashing.» Here concrete is still the building material. The words around it, steel bars and stretching, are the clues.
explain: Concrete can also describe an idea. In «concrete evidence», concrete means real and definite, something you can see or touch, not just an idea. Its opposite is abstract, like kindness, which names a feeling rather than a thing.
fact: Concrete is a hard building material made by mixing cement, sand, small stones and water.
fact: Wet concrete is soft and can be poured, then it sets very hard.
fact: As an adjective, concrete means made of concrete, as in a concrete wall or a concrete path.
fact: Concrete can also mean real and definite, something you can see or touch, as in «concrete objects».
fact: A concrete noun names something you can see or touch, like table. An abstract noun names an idea, like kindness.
exercise: «A house's foundations are concrete under the walls.» What is concrete here? | A kind of idea | A type of glass | *A hard building material | A soft, wet mud from the river
exercise: «Stone and concrete are very strong when squashed but crack easily when stretched.» When does concrete crack easily? | When it is squashed | When it is cold and dry | When it is painted | *When it is stretched
exercise: Which word is a concrete noun, naming something you can see or touch? | kindness | freedom | *table | courage
test: «Reinforced concrete has steel bars inside it so it can resist stretching.» What is inside reinforced concrete? | *Steel bars | Glass panels | Sand and water | Wooden planks
test: The phrase «concrete evidence» means what? | *Real evidence you can see or touch | Evidence that is only a guess | Evidence that is very soft | Evidence written in a poem
exam: «A cement mixer lorry keeps its drum turning so the concrete does not set.» What would happen if the concrete set in the drum? | It would float away | *It would harden | It would turn into a bird | It would become a sweet
